static _Alignas(8) uint8_t br_s53163c5e066ca431[34096] =
"\377\377\377\377\377\377\377\377\035\205\000\000\000\000\000\000# brine quick reference\012\012brine"
" is a compiled, verified, natively parallel language with Python-like syntax.\012Blocks use `:` plus"
" 4-space indentation. Comments start with `#`.\012\012## Programs and commands\012\012```\012brine n"
"ew hello && cd hello    # create a project (brine.toml, src/main.br, AGENTS.md)\012brine run        "
"              # build + run          brine run file.br -- args\012brine check                    # t"
"ype-check + prove   brine check --report\012brine check --typed            # also require a written "
"type on every variable\012brine test                     # run test blocks      brine test \042name "
"filter\042\012brine fmt                      # format               brine fmt --check\012brine run -"
"-ranks 4            # 4 cooperating processes (world module)\012brine debug file.br            # deb"
"ug build in gdb/lldb: break file.br:12, step, print x\012brine explain E0601            # explain an"
"y error code\012brine run --target wasm        # WebAssembly, run with Node.js (needs wasi-sdk, $WAS"
"I_SDK_PATH)\012brine build --target web -o d  # d/: index.html + brine.js + program.wasm, for a brow"
"ser\012```\012Add `--json` to any command for one JSON object per line. `brine fmt` leaves\012lines "
"between `# fmt: off` and `# fmt: on` comments, and number tables written\012in rows that don't fit o"
"n one line, as they are.\012WebAssembly runs on one thread (`par` runs in order, same results) and a"
"s one\012`world` rank; in a browser there are no files or processes.\012\012## Values and types\012\012"
"| Type | Examples |\012|---|---|\012| `int` (= `i64`), `i8 i16 i32 i128 u8 u16 u32 u64 u128` | `42`,"
" `1_000_000`, `0xff`, `0b1010` |\012| `float` (= `f64`), `f32` | `3.14`, `1e-9` |\012| `bool` | `tru"
"e`, `false` |\012| `str` | `\042hi\134n\042`, `f\042x = {x}, pi = {pi:.3f}\042` |\012| arrays `[T]` "
"| `[1, 2, 3]`, `[0.0] * n`, `[i * i for i in 0..n if i % 2 == 0]` |\012| maps `{K: V}` | `{\042ann\042"
": 31, \042bob\042: 27}`, `var m: {str: int} = {}` |\012| tuples `(A, B)` | `(1, \042one\042)`, `pair"
".0` |\012| structs | `Point(x=1, y=2)` or `Point(1, 2)` |\012\012* No implicit conversions. Convert "
"explicitly: `float(i)`, `int(x)` (truncates),\012  `i32(n)` (checked), `str(v)`, `int(\04242\042)` ("
"parses), `float(\0422.5\042)`. A literal\012  takes the type it is converted to: `u64(0xcbf29ce48422"
"2325)` is fine, `u8(300)`\012  is a compile-time error.\012* Literals adapt to context: `let b: u8 ="
" 200`, `let f: f32 = 1`,\012  `let big: i128 = 10_000_000_000_000_000_000_000`. Integer literals go "
"up to\012  `u128`'s maximum (2^128 - 1); above `i128`'s maximum only a `u128` holds them.\012* `i128"
"` and `u128` need a 64-bit target (they're a GCC/Clang feature). The prover\012  can't remove their "
"overflow checks; those always run.\012* Integer overflow, out-of-range conversions, division by zero"
" and\012  out-of-bounds indexing stop the program with a clear message (a *panic*),\012  unless the "
"compiler proved they can't happen, in which case the check is removed.\012  Only the first panic is "
"reported, even when many `par` threads fail at once.\012* Every thread, `main` included, has a 64 MB"
" stack; recursion deeper than that\012  panics with \042stack overflow (recursion too deep)\042. `br"
"ine run` gives\012  WebAssembly the same; in a browser the engine allows less (about 10,000 to\012  "
"100,000 calls, depending on the frames).\012* Integer `/` rounds down like Python's `//` (`-7 / 2 =="
" -4`), and `%` takes the sign of\012  the right side (`-7 % 2 == 1`). Use `float(a) / float(b)` for "
"a fractional result.\012* Bit operators on integers: `&` (and), `|` (or), `^` (xor), `~` (not), `<<`"
", `>>`.\012  Unlike `+` and `*`, `<<` doesn't check for overflow: bits shifted past the type's\012  "
"width are dropped (`u64(1) << 63` is 2^63, `3 << 63` is `-2^63`). `>>` keeps the\012  sign of signed"
" types (`-8 >> 1 == -4`) and fills unsigned ones with zeros. A shift\012  by a negative amount or by"
" the type's width or more panics. Each has a compound\012  assignment: `x &= m`, `x |= m`, `x ^= m`,"
" `x <<= 2`, `x >>= 2`.\012* Methods of integers (any width; the result has the receiver's type):\012"
"  `a.wrapping_add(b)`, `wrapping_sub`, `wrapping_mul` and `a.wrapping_neg()` wrap\012  around instea"
"d of panicking (hashes, random number generators); `-x` on an\012  unsigned type is an error, `x.wra"
"pping_neg()` is the two's complement.\012  `x.rotate_left(n)` / `rotate_right(n)` rotate the bits (`"
"n` modulo the width).\012  `x.count_ones()`, `x.leading_zeros()`, `x.trailing_zeros()` give an `int`"
"\012  (the width for 0: `u64(0).trailing_zeros() == 64`).\012* Float bits: `x.to_bits()` is the IEEE"
" 754 encoding of a `float` as a `u64` (of\012  an `f32` as a `u32`); `float.from_bits(u)` and `f32.f"
"rom_bits(u)` go back.\012* `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` work on numbers, strings (byte order), enums without"
" data\012  (declaration order) and tuples and structs whose parts all support them (not\012  on `boo"
"l`: write `a and not b` for `b < a`). Those\012  compare part by part, in field order, like Python: "
"`(1, \042b\042) < (2, \042a\042)`. A NaN\012  makes `<` false, so in a tuple it's false whenever the"
" NaN part decides. The same\012  types work with `sort()` (stable; NaN sorts after every number), `m"
"in`/`max` of\012  two (ties keep the first) and `min(... for ...)`/`max(...)`; `par min`/`par max`\012"
"  need numbers.\012* Values are **values**: `let b = a` copies (cheaply, copy-on-write). Changing `b"
"`\012  never changes `a`. To share a variable on purpose, use `ref` (see Functions).\012\012## Bindi"
"ngs\012\012```python\012let x = 10              # immutable\012var total = 0           # mutable\012"
"total += x\012let (q, r) = (17 / 5, 17 % 5)   # tuple unpacking\012let (a, (b, c)) = (1, (2, 3))   #"
" nests\012u, v = v, u % v         # assign several `var` places at once; parentheses optional\012xs["
"i], xs[j] = xs[j], xs[i]     # every value is computed first, then stored\012lo, _ = bounds         "
" # `_` drops a value\012var names: [str] = []   # annotate when the type can't be inferred\012let n:"
" i32 = 7          # ...or whenever you like\012for i: int in 0..3:     # loop variables too: `for k:"
" str, v: int in m.items():`\012    total += i\012const LIMIT = 100       # compile-time constant (to"
"p level)\012const BASE = [3, 4, 5, 6]       # read-only arrays too; `len(BASE)` is a constant\012```"
"\012In `x, y = ...` the places can't overlap, and an index can't read a variable\012the same line as"
"signs (`i, xs[i] = ...` is error E0405): the result would\012depend on the order of the stores.\012\012"
"Types are optional, Python style. To require them, put `typed = true` under\012`[check]` in brine.to"
"ml (or pass `--typed`): then every `let`, `var` and `for`\012variable needs a written type (error E0"
"337 tells you which one to add).\012No shadowing: a name can be declared only once per function. `_`"
" ignores a\012value (`let (q, _) = ...`, `for _ in 0..n:`); it may repeat and can't be read.\012\012"
"## Control flow\012\012```python\012if x < 0:\012    print(\042negative\042)\012elif x == 0:\012    "
"print(\042zero\042)\012else:\012    print(\042positive\042)\012\012let sign = \042neg\042 if x < 0 e"
"lse \042non-neg\042   # conditional expression\012\012for i in 0..n:          # 0, 1, ..., n-1      "
"  (also: 0..=n, range(a, b, step))\012    pass\012for x in xs:            # iterates over a snapshot"
" of xs\012    pass\012for i, x in enumerate(xs):  # unpack tuples: (0, xs[0]), (1, xs[1]), ...\012  "
"  pass\012for i, (k, v) in enumerate(pairs):  # nested tuples too (also in comprehensions)\012    pa"
"ss\012for c in \042h\303\251llo\042:       # each UTF-8 character, as a one-character str\012    pas"
"s\012while k < n:\012    k += 1              # break / continue work as usual\012```\012Comparisons "
"chain: `0 <= i < n`. Logic is `and`, `or`, `not`.\012\012## Functions\012\012```python\012fn area(w:"
" float, h: float) -> float:\012    return w * h\012\012fn fill(ref xs: [int], v: int):      # `ref` "
"param: changes the caller's variable\012    for i in 0..len(xs):\012        xs[i] = v\012\012fn coun"
"tdown(mut n: int):            # `mut` param: a local copy the function may change\012    while n > 0"
":\012        n -= 1\012\012fn main():\012    var data = [0] * 10\012    fill(ref data, 7)           "
"     # the caller says `ref` too\012    let d = ref data                 # `d` is another name for `"
"data`\012    d[0] = 1                         # changes `data`\012```\012`let r = ref p.field` names"
" a field the same way (`let b = ref self.board` in a\012method: no copy, and changes go to `self.boa"
"rd`). A reference names a variable or\012a field path, not an element (`ref xs[i]`) or a function's "
"result.\012Arguments are passed by value (cheaply: arrays and strings are copy-on-write).\012`x = f("
"x)` (also `xs[i] = f(xs[i])`, `x = x.step()`) moves x's value into `f`, so\012an `f` that starts wit"
"h `var t = x` changes it in place instead of copying it\012(unless something else shares the value)."
"\012References can't be stored in structs or arrays or returned, so they never dangle.\012`main` may"
" return `int` (the exit code) or a `Result` (see below). Parameter types are required;\012local type"
"s are inferred.\012\012## Structs and methods\012\012```python\012struct Particle:\012    pos: float"
"\012    vel: float\012\012    fn step(ref self, dt: float):    # method that modifies its receiver\012"
"        self.pos += self.vel * dt\012\012    fn energy(self) -> float:\012        return 0.5 * self."
"vel * self.vel\012\012    fn at_rest(pos: float) -> Particle:   # no `self`: call as Particle.at_res"
"t(1.0)\012        return Particle(pos=pos, vel=0.0)\012\012var p = Particle(pos=0.0, vel=2.0)\012p.s"
"tep(0.1)\012print(p, p.energy())                 # Particle(pos=0.2, vel=2.0) 2.0\012```\012\012## E"
"nums and `match`\012\012```python\012enum Color:            # no data: a plain tag\012    Red\012   "
" Green\012\012enum Shape:\012    Circle(radius: float)\012    Rect(w: float, h: float)\012    Empty\012"
"\012    fn area(self) -> float:          # enums can have methods\012        match self:\012        "
"    case Shape.Circle(r):\012                return 3.14 * r * r\012            case Shape.Rect(w, h"
"):\012                return w * h\012            case Shape.Empty:\012                return 0.0\012"
"\012enum Expr:                           # recursive enums are fine\012    Num(value: int)\012    Ad"
"d(left: Expr, right: Expr)\012\012let s = Shape.Rect(w=2.0, h=3.0)     # positional or keyword field"
"s\012print(s, s.area(), s == Shape.Empty) # Shape.Rect(w=2.0, h=3.0) 6.0 false\012\012print(int(Colo"
"r.Green))              # 1: an enum without data converts to its position\012print(Color.from_int(0)"
", Color.from_int(7))   # Some(Color.Red) None\012```\012\012Patterns: `_`, a name (captures), int/st"
"r/bool literals, tuples `(a, 0)`,\012alternatives `1 | 2 | 3`, and variants `Shape.Rect(w, _)` / `Ex"
"pr.Add(left=l)`\012(nested to any depth). A guard adds a condition: `case x if x < 0:`.\012A constan"
"t matches its value: `case OP_ADD:` (integer, string and bool constants).\012Alternatives may bind n"
"ames when each binds the same ones with the same types:\012`case Shape.Circle(n) | Shape.Square(n):`"
" (E0321 otherwise). With a guard,\012each alternative is tried in turn: `case (x, _) | (_, x) if x >"
" 5:` matches `(1, 9)`.\012Every `match` must handle every value (error E0320 shows one it misses);\012"
"end with `case _:` when listing values isn't possible. Variant data is read with\012`match`, never w"
"ith `.field`.\012\012Cost: an enum whose variants hold at most 64 bytes and that can't contain itsel"
"f\012(`Shape`, `Option[int]`, `Result[int, str]`) is stored in place, like a struct: making\012one a"
"llocates nothing. A recursive enum (`Expr`) or a bigger one is a pointer to a\012shared, reference-c"
"ounted box. `match (a, b)` on variables doesn't copy them. A `match`\012whose cases test one value a"
"gainst four or more close-together integers or\012variants jumps straight to the right case, in ever"
"y kind of build.\012\012## Generics, `Option` and `Result`\012\012```python\012struct Pair[A, B]:\012"
"    first: A\012    second: B\012\012fn first[T](xs: [T]) -> Option[T]:     # type parameters in bra"
"ckets\012    if len(xs) == 0:\012        return None\012    return Some(xs[0])\012\012fn parse_bit(s"
": str) -> Result[int, str]:\012    if s == \0420\042:\012        return Ok(0)\012    if s == \0421\042"
":\012        return Ok(1)\012    return Err(f\042not a bit: {s}\042)\012\012let p = Pair(first=1, se"
"cond=\042one\042)     # type arguments are inferred...\012let q = Pair[int, str](first=2, second=\042"
"two\042)   # ...or written out\012let none: Option[int] = None           # `None` alone needs a know"
"n type\012match first([3, 4]):\012    case Some(x):\012        print(x)\012    case None:\012       "
" print(\042empty\042)\012print(parse_bit(\0421\042).unwrap(), parse_bit(\042x\042).unwrap_or(-1))   "
"# 1 -1\012```\012\012`x\077` unwraps an `Option` or `Result`: it gives the value inside `Some`/`Ok`,"
" or\012returns the `None`/`Err` from the current function at once (the function must\012return `Opti"
"on[...]`, or `Result[..., E]` with the same `E`):\012\012```python\012fn load(path: str) -> Result[i"
"nt, str]:\012    let text = read_file(path)\077          # Err(...) is returned to the caller\012   "
" return parse_bit(text.strip())\012\012fn main() -> Result[(), str]:           # an Err prints \042e"
"rror: ...\042 and exits with 1\012    print(load(\042bit.txt\042)\077)\012    return Ok(())\012```\012"
"`main` may also return `Result[int, E]`: `Ok(n)` exits with code `n`.\012\012`Option[T]` (`Some(valu"
"e)`, `None`) and `Result[T, E]` (`Ok(value)`, `Err(error)`)\012are always available. Methods: `is_so"
"me()`, `is_none()`, `is_ok()`, `is_err()`,\012`unwrap()` (panics on `None`, and on `Err` with the er"
"ror), `unwrap_or(default)`, `ok()` (Result to Option).\012Each use of a generic function or type wit"
"h new type arguments makes a\012specialized copy, so generic code is as fast as hand-written code. A"
" generic\012function's body is checked for each set of type arguments it's used with:\012`largest[T]"
"` that compares with `>` works for `int`, `str` or a tuple, and is an\012error for a struct with an "
"array field. In the same way, `T(x)` converts to what `T` stands for, like\012`int(x)` or `f32(x)`: "
"`fn mean[E](xs: [E]) -> E` can return `total / E(len(xs))`.\012\012Methods can have type parameters "
"of their own, next to their type's:\012\012```python\012struct Box[T]:\012    v: T\012\012    fn pai"
"r[U](self, u: U) -> (T, U):\012        return (self.v, u)\012\012let b = Box(v=1)\012print(b.pair(\042"
"x\042), b.pair[float](2.5))   # (1, \042x\042) (1, 2.5)\012```\012\012## Arrays\012\012```python\012"
"var xs = [3, 1, 2]\012xs.push(5)              # append\012let last = xs.pop()     # remove and retur"
"n the last element (xs.pop(i): at i)\012xs.insert(0, 9)         # put 9 at index 0, shifting the res"
"t right\012xs.extend([7, 8])       # append every element\012xs.sort()               # anything `<` "
"works on; stable\012xs.reverse()\012xs.clear()\012print(len(xs), xs == [3, 1, 2], 3 in xs, xs.index("
"3))  # index: Option[int]\012print([3, 1, 3].index(3, 1))    # Some(2): searching from index 1 (0 to"
" len, else a panic)\012print(xs.contains(3))   # the same as `3 in xs`\012print(xs[1:3], xs[:2], xs["
"2:])   # slices are copies; out of range panics\012let both = xs + [4, 5]  # a new array; `xs += ys`"
" extends xs in place\012```\012There are no negative indexes: write `xs[len(xs) - 1]`. A statement c"
"an't both\012change a variable and read it elsewhere (`print(xs.pop(), xs)` is an error), so\012eval"
"uation order never matters. A call in another call's arguments runs first\012(`t.add(t.leaf(1), 2)` "
"is fine), and different fields are different places\012(`both(ref self.a, ref self.b)`, `self.items["
"self.rng.below(n)]`).\012\012In release builds, a `for j in lo..hi` loop that indexes arrays at `j`,"
" `j + x`,\012`x + j` or `j - x` (`x` a literal or a variable the loop doesn't change), calls\012no f"
"unctions of the program and doesn't change those arrays' lengths checks the first and last index onc"
"e,\012before the loop, and then runs without per-element checks, so the C compiler can\012vectorize "
"it. If that test fails the loop runs checked as written, panicking at\012the same element it always "
"would.\012\012Cost: an empty array allocates nothing for its elements until the first push, so\012ta"
"king an inner array out to change it (`var w = xss[i]; xss[i] = []`, then\012`xss[i] = w`) costs one"
" small allocation. A local `var a = [x] * n` with `n` a\012literal (at most 1 KB of numbers or `bool"
"`s; 4 KB per function), in a function\012without `par`, that is only indexed, assigned at an index a"
"nd measured with `len`\012lives in the stack frame: no allocation at all (a traversal stack, a histo"
"gram).\012\012## Strings\012\012Strings are immutable UTF-8. `len(s)`, `s[i]` and slices count **byt"
"es**;\012`s[i]` is the byte at `i` as a one-byte string: a character for ASCII text\012(the common c"
"ase), a piece of one otherwise, so scan text byte by byte and\012compare with ASCII (`s[i] == \042 \042"
"`). A slice must not cut a character in two:\012`\042a\303\251\042[0:2]` panics. `for c in s` / `s.c"
"hars()` walk real characters.\012\012```python\012let s = \042hello, brine\042\012print(s[0], s[7:],"
" s + \042!\042, \042ri\042 in s, s.contains(\042ri\042), f\042{s} has {len(s)} bytes\042)\012print(s"
".find(\042brine\042))                    # Some(7); None if absent\012print(s.find(\042e\042, 2))   "
"                  # Some(11): searching from byte 2 (0 to len(s))\012print(s.split(\042, \042), \042"
"a b  c\042.split())    # [\042hello\042, \042brine\042], [\042a\042, \042b\042, \042c\042]\012print("
"\042-\042.join([\042a\042, \042b\042]), \042ab\042.repeat(3), \042ab\042 * 3, s.replace(\042l\042, \042"
"L\042))\012print(s.starts_with(\042he\042), s.ends_with(\042!\042), s.count(\042l\042))\012print(\042"
"  x \042.strip(), \042  x\042.lstrip(), \042x  \042.rstrip(), s.upper(), s.lower())\012print(\042a\134"
"nb\042.lines(), s.chars(), s.bytes())  # [str], [str], [u8]\012print(\04242\042.is_digit(), \042ab\042"
".is_alpha(), \042 \042.is_space(), \042a1\042.is_alnum())\012print(ord(\042a\042), chr(97))         "
"          # 97 \042a\042\012```\012Text over several lines goes between `\042\042\042` quotes (also "
"`f\042\042\042...\042\042\042`). A line\012break right after the opening quotes is dropped, and so i"
"s the indentation all\012the lines share (the closing quotes' line counts, blank lines don't); `\134"
"` at the\012end of a line joins it with the next:\012\012```python\012fn usage(name: str) -> str:\012"
"    return f\042\042\042\012        usage: {name} [--verbose] FILE\012          --verbose   say more"
"\012        \042\042\042\012# usage(\042app\042) == \042usage: app [--verbose] FILE\134n  --verbose "
"  say more\134n\042\012```\012An f-string field holds any expression, strings included: `f\042{d[\042"
"key\042]}\042`,\012`f\042{\042, \042.join(names)}\042`.\012\012Case conversion and `is_alpha`/`is_sp"
"ace` follow Unicode, as in Python\012(`\042stra\303\237e\042.upper()` is `\042STRASSE\042`); `is_dig"
"it` is `0`-`9`, the digits `int()`\012reads. `str(x)` converts any\012printable value; `int(\04242\042"
")` and `float(\0422.5\042)` parse (and panic on bad input).\012For text from outside, `s.to_int()` a"
"nd `s.to_float()` give `None` instead:\012\012```python\012match line.to_int():                     "
" # Option[int]; blanks around it are fine\012    case Some(n):\012        print(n + 1)\012    case N"
"one:\012        print(\042not a number\042)\012\042200\042.to_int[u8]()                        # Som"
"e(200): other types; None if out of range\012\0422.5\042.to_float[f32]()                     # Some("
"2.5)\012str.from_utf8(bytes)                      # Result[str, str]: Err names the bad byte\012```\012"
"Both accept `_` between digits (`\0421_000\042`), as `int()` and `float()` do.\012\012Format specs i"
"n f-strings, as in Python: `{x:.2f}` (fixed), `{x:.3e}`\012(scientific), `{x:g}` / `{x:.10g}` (gener"
"al: the shorter of the two, trailing\012zeros dropped), `{n:,}` / `{x:,.2f}` (thousands separators: "
"`1,234,567.89`),\012`{n:x}` / `{n:b}` (hex, binary), a width `{n:8}` (`<8` to left-align, `08` to pa"
"d\012with zeros) and combinations like `{x:>12,.2f}`.\012\012## Maps\012\012```python\012var ages = "
"{\042ann\042: 31, \042bob\042: 27}   # {str: int}; remembers insertion order\012ages[\042cy\042] = 4"
"0                     # insert or replace\012ages[\042ann\042] += 1                    # missing key"
"s panic: check with `in` first\012if \042bob\042 in ages:\012    print(ages[\042bob\042])\012print(a"
"ges.get(\042zed\042))              # Option[int]: None\012print(ages.get(\042zed\042, 0))           "
"# 0 (default)\012ages.remove(\042bob\042)                  # true if it was there\012for name in age"
"s:                   # keys, in insertion order (a snapshot:\012    print(name, ages[name])         "
"# the loop body may change the map)\012print(ages.keys(), ages.values(), ages.items(), len(ages))\012"
"```\012\012Keys can be integers, `bool`, `str`, enums, or arrays/tuples/structs of those\012(not flo"
"ats). Maps are values like everything else: `let b = a` copies (lazily).\012\012## Built-ins\012\012"
"`print(a, b, ...)` (`sep=\042 \042` goes between the values and `end=\042\134n\042` after\012them: `"
"print(x, end=\042\042)`), `len`, `abs`, `min(a, b)`, `max(a, b)`, `sqrt`, `sin`, `cos`,\012`tan`, `a"
"sin`, `acos`, `atan`, `atan2(y, x)`, `sinh`, `cosh`, `tanh`, `exp`, `exp2`,\012`log`, `log2`, `log10"
"`, `cbrt`, `pow(x, y)`, `hypot(x, y)` (on `float` or `f32`),\012`floor`, `ceil`, `round`, `clock()` "
"(seconds),\012`args()` (the command-line arguments as `[str]`, without the program's name:\012`brine"
" run app.br -- a b` gives `[\042a\042, \042b\042]`), `exit(code)`, `panic(msg)`,\012`eprint(...)` (p"
"rint to stderr), `ord(c)`, `chr(n)`, `enumerate(xs)`, `flush()`.\012On floats, `min` and `max` ignor"
"e a NaN (the other argument wins; both NaN gives\012NaN) and order `-0.0` below `0.0`, the same on e"
"very target.\012\012The math functions give the same bits on every platform, natively and in\012WebA"
"ssembly: brine has its own math library (from musl) rather than the C\012library's, whose last bits "
"differ between systems.\012\012`print` output to a terminal or a pipe is written at the end of each "
"line; to a\012file it is buffered (and written at exit, or when `eprint`, `run` or `flush()`\012need"
"s it out). `flush()` writes out what is buffered, such as a prompt printed\012with `end=\042\042`.\012"
"\012Standard input, from a terminal, a pipe or a file:\012\012```python\012while true:\012    match "
"input(\042> \042):                  # Option[str]: a line without its \042\134n\042\012        case "
"Some(line):                # (or \042\134r\134n\042), None at the end of input\012            print("
"line.upper())\012        case None:\012            break\012let all = read_stdin()                  "
"# Result[str, str]: everything that is left\012```\012`input(prompt)` writes the prompt first. Text "
"that isn't UTF-8 becomes U+FFFD in\012`input()` and an `Err` from `read_stdin()`. On Windows a conso"
"le's input is read\012as UTF-8 too. A web page has no stdin: `input()` gives `None` there.\012\012Fi"
"les and processes (all failures are values, never crashes):\012\012```python\012match read_file(\042"
"in.txt\042):              # Result[str, str]; text must be UTF-8\012    case Ok(text):\012        pr"
"int(len(text.lines()))\012    case Err(e):\012        eprint(e)                       # \042cannot r"
"ead in.txt: No such file or directory\042\012write_file(\042out.txt\042, \042hi\134n\042)          #"
" Result[(), str]\012append_file(\042log.txt\042, \042one more\134n\042)   # Result[(), str]: creates"
" the file if needed\012file_exists(\042out.txt\042)                  # bool\012getenv(\042HOME\042) "
"                         # Option[str]\012run([\042cc\042, \042-O2\042, \042-o\042, \042app\042, \042"
"app.c\042])  # Result[int, str]: Ok(exit code), Err if it can't start\012```\012`write_file`, `appen"
"d_file`, `run`, `input` and `read_stdin` can't be used inside\012`par` (their effects would happen i"
"n an unpredictable order), nor can a function\012that calls one of them, directly or through other f"
"unctions. `exit` can't be\012used in a `par` body either.\012\012The `os` module (built in, no impor"
"t; errors are `Err` values with the OS's\012message, e.g. `\042The system cannot find the file speci"
"fied. (os error 2)\042`):\012\012```python\012os.list_dir(path)       # Result[[str], str]: entry na"
"mes (not paths), sorted, no \042.\042 / \042..\042\012os.is_dir(path), os.is_file(path)            #"
" bool\012os.make_dir(path)       # Result[(), str]: creates missing parents; Ok if it exists\012os.r"
"emove_file(path), os.rename(src, dst)    # Result[(), str]; rename replaces dst\012os.cwd(), os.temp"
"_dir() # str (temp_dir has no trailing separator)\012os.set_cwd(path)        # Result[(), str]: chan"
"ge the working directory\012os.write_bytes(path, b) # Result[(), str]: write a [u8] as a binary file"
"\012os.append_bytes(path, b)                     # Result[(), str]: add to the end\012os.read_bytes("
"path)     # Result[[u8], str]: a file's bytes, whatever they are\012os.exe_path()           # str: t"
"he absolute path of the running program\012os.abs_path(path)       # Result[str, str]: canonical; on"
" Windows `\134\134\077\134C:\134...`\012os.name(), os.arch()    # \042windows\042/\042macos\042/\042"
"linux\042..., \042x86_64\042/\042aarch64\042...\012os.pid(), os.cpu_count()                     # in"
"t (cpu_count >= 1)\012os.thread_count()       # int: threads `par` uses (BRINE_THREADS / --threads; "
"1 on wasm)\012os.sleep(0.5)           # seconds\012os.is_terminal(2)       # is fd 1 (stdout) / 2 (s"
"tderr) / 0 a terminal\077\012os.enable_ansi()        # Windows: let the console show color codes\012"
"os.free_ports(n)        # Result[[int], str]: n distinct free localhost TCP ports\012let p = os.spaw"
"n([\042cc\042, \042x.c\042], {\042LANG\042: \042C\042}, true, false)\077  # Result[int, str]: a hand"
"le\012#   env: added to this process's environment; capture: collect stdout/stderr;\012#   stdin: fa"
"lse gives the child an empty stdin. cmd[0] is searched on PATH.\012os.wait(p)              # int: ex"
"it code (101 if killed by a signal)\012os.poll(p)              # Option[int]: exit code if it has ex"
"ited\012os.kill(p)              # stop it\012let (out, err) = os.output(p)   # captured text once it"
" has exited (\042\042 if not captured)\012```\012`os.spawn`, `wait`, `poll`, `kill`, `make_dir`, `re"
"move_file`, `rename`,\012`set_cwd`, `write_bytes`, `append_bytes`, `sleep` and `enable_ansi` can't b"
"e used\012inside `par`, directly or through a function. Results of `os` calls (except\012`os.name()`"
"/`os.arch()`) count as rank-dependent for `world` deadlock checks.\012\012`embed(\042help.txt\042)` "
"is a `str` with the file's contents, read when the program is\012compiled (path: a string literal, r"
"elative to the file containing the call;\012`\134r\134n` becomes `\134n`). It works in `const`s too:"
" `const HELP = embed(\042help.txt\042)`.\012Reductions over generators: `sum(x * x for x in xs)`, `p"
"roduct`, `min`, `max`,\012`any(x < 0 for x in xs)`, `all(...)`. `min`/`max` of an empty sequence pan"
"ic.\012`first(Some(x) if p(x) else None for x in xs)` is the first `Some` of a generator\012of optio"
"ns (or of an array of them), or `None`; it stops at that element.\012\012## Parallelism (data-race f"
"ree by construction)\012\012```python\012let ys = par [f(x) for x in xs]              # parallel map"
"\012let total = par sum(x * x for x in xs)        # parallel reduction\012let at = par first(Some(i)"
" if ok(i) else None for i in 0..n)  # parallel search\012var out = [0.0] * n\012par for i in 0..n:  "
"                          # parallel loop\012    out[i] = xs[i] * 2.0                      # outer a"
"rrays: only out[i] (or out[k*i + c])\012let (a, b) = par(work(1), work(2))            # fork-join\012"
"```\012Rules: inside a `par` body, outer variables are read-only, except that\012`par for i in lo..h"
"i` (over a range) may write `arr[i]` (exactly the loop\012variable) of an outer array, or `arr[k * i"
" + c]` with integer constants\012`k >= 1` and `c`: every write of `arr` in the loop uses the same `k"
"`, and its\012offsets `c` aren't a multiple of `k` apart, so no two iterations write the same\012ele"
"ment. The loop may also read `arr[k * i + c]` at an element it writes, or at\012one no iteration wri"
"tes. So in-place red-black sweeps work:\012```python\012par for i in 0..n:\012    a[2 * i + 1] = 0.5"
" * (a[2 * i] + a[2 * i + 2])   # odd from even\012```\012Reductions are deterministic: same result b"
"its on any machine and thread count.\012They fold leaves of 16 consecutive elements left to right, t"
"hen combine the leaves\012in a balanced pairwise tree (so `par sum` of floats can differ in the last"
" bits from\012a sequential `sum`, usually by being more accurate). Integer overflow of any partial\012"
"result in that order traps. Only the scheduling adapts: a reduction over a few\012expensive elements"
" still uses all the threads. `par any`/`par all` stop\012starting new work once an element decides t"
"he result; the elements before it\012always run, so a panic the sequential loop would reach still ha"
"ppens.\012`par first(...)` is the lowest-index `Some`, like `first`, on any thread count;\012the ele"
"ments before it all run, those after it may not.\012`BRINE_THREADS=n` or `brine run --threads n` set"
"s the thread count.\012Cost: reference counts use locked (atomic) instructions only while a `par` co"
"nstruct\012is running; the rest of the program updates them with plain ones. In `par for i`,\012`out"
"[i] = f(out[i])` hands `out[i]` to `f` without copying it, as outside `par`.\012\012**GPUs.** `par f"
"or`, `par [...]` and `par` reductions also run on a GPU (NVIDIA\012through CUDA, Apple silicon throu"
"gh Metal) when the runtime expects that to be\012faster (big constructs with much work per byte copi"
"ed); the results are the same\012bits, and a failing check panics as on the CPU. On Apple GPUs, f64 "
"code stays on\012the CPU (they have no f64).\012Nothing to write or install beyond the driver: the c"
"ompiler makes a kernel of every\012body that only uses int/float arithmetic, comparisons, branches, "
"loops, structs,\012tuples, reads of outer arrays and scalars, `out[i]` writes, `sqrt`, `abs`, `floor"
"`,\012`ceil`, `round`, `min`, `max`, and calls to functions that do the same (which may\012use local"
" arrays kept in the stack frame, see Arrays); on NVIDIA GPUs also\012`i128`/`u128` arithmetic (excep"
"t reductions of them, conversions to and from\012floats, and passing them to functions). Strings, ma"
"ps, other new arrays, printing,\012`sin`/`exp`/`log`/`pow` and the like keep a body on the CPU.\012`"
"BRINE_GPU=off|on|auto` (default `auto`; `on` uses the GPU whenever it can),\012`BRINE_GPU_LOG=1` pri"
"nts where each construct ran and why. With `--ranks`, the\012k-th rank on a machine uses its k-th GP"
"U and the ranks beyond its GPUs stay on the\012CPU (ranks sharing a GPU queue for it); `on` shares t"
"he GPUs out instead.\012\012## Multiple processes and machines: `world`\012\012```python\012fn main("
"):\012    let rank = world.rank()           # 0 .. world.size()-1\012    let local = sum(i for i in "
"0..1000 if i % world.size() == rank)\012    let total = world.sum(local)      # every rank gets the "
"total\012    if rank == 0:\012        print(total)\012    let left = world.shift(rank, 1)       # se"
"nd to rank + 1, receive from rank - 1\012    let up = world.shift(rank, 1, -1)     # no wrapping aro"
"und: rank 0 gets -1\012    let inbox = world.exchange([rank] * world.size())   # xs[j] goes to rank "
"j\012    let sums = world.sum([1.0, float(rank)])            # element by element\012    let done = "
"world.all(rank >= 0)                     # also world.any\012```\012API: `rank()`, `size()`, `barrie"
"r()`, `broadcast(root, v)`, `sum(v)`, `min(v)`,\012`max(v)` (on `int`, `float`, `[int]`, `[float]`),"
" `any(b)`, `all(b)`,\012`gather_all(v) -> [T]`, `shift(v, by)`, `shift(v, by, edge)`, `exchange(xs) "
"-> [T]`.\012Message types: integers and floats of any width (`[u8]` travels as bytes), `bool`,\012`s"
"tr`, and arrays of those (`[[float]]`, ...). A float `sum` adds the ranks'\012values in a fixed tree"
": the same result on every rank and every run.\012Run with `brine run --ranks N` (each rank gets cor"
"es / N `par` threads unless\012`--threads` says otherwise). Without it, `world.size() == 1`. If a ra"
"nk fails,\012`brine run` stops the others and exits with that rank's exit code.\012\012**No deadlock"
"s, by construction.** Every rank takes part in every `world` call,\012and the compiler checks that a"
"ll ranks make the same calls in the same order:\012a call under a condition that depends on `world.r"
"ank()` (or received values,\012`clock()`, ...), or after a rank-dependent `return`, `break` or `exit"
"`, is an\012error (E0510). Results of `sum`/`min`/`max`/`any`/`all`/`broadcast`/\012`gather_all` are"
" the same on every rank, so loops like\012`while world.max(err) > tol:` are fine. At run time, each "
"message also carries\012its call site, so a mismatch is a clear panic naming both calls.\012\012## P"
"roofs: rules that can't be broken\012\012```python\012fn clamp(x: int, lo: int, hi: int) -> int:\012"
"    requires lo <= hi                 # every caller must prove this\012    ensures lo <= result <= "
"hi        # proven for every return\012    if x < lo:\012        return lo\012    if x > hi:\012    "
"    return hi\012    return x\012\012fn index_of(xs: [int], target: int) -> int:\012    ensures -1 <"
"= result < len(xs)\012    var lo = 0\012    var hi = len(xs)\012    while lo < hi:\012        invari"
"ant 0 <= lo <= hi <= len(xs)     # holds before every iteration\012        let mid = lo + (hi - lo) "
"/ 2\012        if xs[mid] == target:                  # bounds check proven: removed\012            "
"return mid\012        if xs[mid] < target:\012            lo = mid + 1\012        else:\012         "
"   hi = mid\012    return -1\012```\012* `requires`, `ensures`, `invariant` and `prove e` **must** b"
"e proven at compile\012  time; if the prover can't, it's an error listing the facts it knew.\012* `a"
"ssert e` checks at runtime (always, in every build) and is a known fact\012  afterwards. Use it to h"
"and the prover a fact it can't derive itself.\012* Specs may use arithmetic, comparisons, `and`/`or`"
"/`not`, `len`, `abs`, `min`,\012  `max`, fields, indexing, constants and `result`.\012* In `for i in"
" a..b` loops, invariants may mention `i`: they must hold at the start\012  of each iteration (and wi"
"th `i = b` after the loop).\012* Of what a loop changes (down to the fields and array elements it wr"
"ites), the\012  prover knows only what the invariants say; everything else it still knows. A\012  ca"
"ll forgets only what the function may change in its `ref` arguments.\012* The prover handles linear "
"integer arithmetic (`+ - ` and multiplication or\012  division by constants). It never accepts a fal"
"se claim.\012* `brine check --report` lists the runtime checks that remain.\012\012## Modules\012\012"
"```python\012import geometry            # loads geometry.br next to the main file\012import util.str"
"ings as s   # loads util/strings.br\012import ..common            # loads common.br from the directo"
"ry above\012\012let a = geometry.area(2.0, 3.0)\012let p = geometry.Point(x=1, y=2)\012\012from geom"
"etry import Point, area   # use these names directly\012let q = Point(x=3, y=4)\012```\012\012Every "
"module path starts at the main file's directory, in whichever module the\012`import` is written. Eac"
"h leading `..` goes one directory up: `import ..common`,\012`from ..common import Thing`, `import .."
"..shared.util` (two up), and the module\012is named after its last part (`common`, `util`).\012\012`"
"from m import x` takes an item `m` defines or itself imports with `from`, so\012a module can gather "
"names for others (`m.x` finds the same item). Modules `m`\012loads with `import` aren't passed on. A"
" name can't be both\012imported and defined in the same module.\012\012## Tests\012\012```python\012"
"test \042clamp keeps values in range\042:\012    assert clamp(15, 0, 10) == 10\012    assert clamp(-"
"5, 0, 10) == 0\012```\012`test` is a keyword only at the start of a top-level line (and `case` only "
"at\012the start of a line in a `match`), so both are fine as names elsewhere.\012`brine test` runs e"
"ach test in its own process, in parallel. `$BRINE` is set to the\012`brine` running the tests, so a "
"test can run the compiler. A test that runs longer\012than 300 seconds is stopped and fails (`brine "
"test --timeout <seconds>`; 0: no limit).\012\012## C interop\012\012```python\012extern fn rand() ->"
" i32     # declare a C standard library function\012extern fn puts(s: str) -> i32\012```\012Extern f"
"unctions take integers, floats, bools and strings, and return integers,\012floats, bools or nothing;"
" the types must match the C declaration (`int` is\012`i32`, `size_t` is `u64` on 64-bit targets and "
"`u32` in WebAssembly). A `str`\012reaches C as a NUL-terminated `const char *` copy that lives until"
" the call\012returns (a string containing a NUL byte panics). Strings can't be returned.\012\000\000"
"\000";
